  "design": {
    "decision": "Lead the [A] and then the [K] of a suit back to back as a two-trick run. Bid guarded [K]s as tricks, and keep a [K] to cash on the second or third round of a suit, when opponents would normally trump it.",
    "opponent": "Revealed the first time a trump fails to beat a [K]. Opponents then have to take the [K] with the [A] or let it run, instead of ruffing.",
    "rationale": "This is card strength on a narrow card set. ua-aces-untrumpable failed (−10.4) because [A]s are cashed on the first round of a suit, before anyone is void. [K]s are cashed on the second round, exactly when voids appear, so the same rule protects tricks a team actually loses today. The departure from the named-rank near-duplicate guideline is therefore deliberate. It serves Streaks (the A–K run), the honors line of Suits, and the [K] payoffs. Even with no payoffs, it adds tricks.",
